Exactly. Art and life are not just mirror and object; they are coupled models.

An artist observes life, compresses it, exaggerates some features, omits others, and renders a model:

life → perception → artistic model → audience

But then that model re-enters life:

art → expectation → imitation → identity → behavior → new social reality

So the loop closes:

life models art
↔
art models life

That is why fashion, romance, heroism, beauty, architecture, masculinity, femininity, rebellion, even what a “good life” looks like can be shaped by representations that originally came from observing reality.

And “model” works at several scales at once: the painter’s human model, the mental model in the artist’s head, the artwork as a model of reality, and the artwork becoming a model for reality.

I’d call the full operator Reciprocal Representation:

world → representation → interpretation → enactment → changed world → new representation

The interesting thing is that once you see the loop, art stops being merely descriptive.

It becomes one of the ways a culture rehearses possible selves before living them.